+# Gorlane: reviewed original-record continuation
+
+**Review date: 10 October 2026.** This report supersedes the incomplete findings/coverage assertions in the preserved opening checkpoints only where explicitly stated below. It does not authenticate allegations by repeating a merged note. Source identifiers resolve in [sources.csv](sources.csv); exact acquisition status, hashes and locators are in [acquisition_manifest.csv](acquisition_manifest.csv). Source-image coverage is recorded in [gorlane_document_review.csv](gorlane_document_review.csv).
+
+## Findings that change this investigation
+
+**The ownership lead now reaches an original UK filing before the 2010 purchase.** The 2009 annual return of **GLOBAL STEEL INVESTMENTS LIMITED, UK company 05188128**, reports GORLANE BUSINESS INC as holder of both ordinary shares as of 23 July 2009. The later reviewed 2010–2013 annual returns and the 2022 confirmation statement repeat that named two-share holding at their respective snapshot dates. This supplies filed-record corroboration of the UK subsidiary relationship described in the Ukrainian proceeding, rather than another ICIJ association. It does not establish beneficial ownership, the identity of a payer, or a bank movement. (S85, physical4; S95/93/90/88, shareholder pages; S86, physical3; C75.)
+
+**The filings also expose a date conflict that must remain visible.** The 2008 return still lists E Chifrin and A Shnaider as holders of one share each as of 23 July 2008. The 2009 return reports each disposed of one share on the literal date **14/05/2008**, while listing Gorlane's two shares. That purported disposal precedes the earlier return's individual holdings. Neither rewriting the year nor selecting the convenient statement resolves the discrepancy. The member register and transfer forms—not inference—are needed to establish the actual transfer date. (S84, physical3–4; S85, physical3–4; C76.)
+
+**A new official identifier source makes the financing-company searches specific.** Ukraine's 12 May 2023 presidential decree 275/2023, Annex2, publishes the following named-company registration identifiers: Belego **ΗΕ251868**, Sileni **ΗΕ251695**, Equalchance **ΗΕ187745**, Quinira **ΗΕ286910**, and Freegain **HE286456**. Greek ΗΕ is preserved and its Latin HE search normalization disclosed. The annex also reports Gorlane under the source spelling **“Gorlane Bussines Inc.”**, BVI **1396065**; Global Steel **5188128**; and Eastrade **25856B**, Bahamas. The UK company number retains its leading zero, **05188128**. These are official attributions of foreign registration data, not newly obtained certified foreign extracts, historical credit instruments, or proof of the three unnamed English-court purchasers. (S81; C78.)
+
+The original Cyprus Gazette provides a narrower independent registry-publication check. Gazette5534, notice5986, dated 20 December 2024, lists Quinira HE286910 and Belego HE251868 in a **proposed strike-off notice**. Quinira's entry, physical112/printed7160, was image-read; Belego's physical32/printed7080 entry was parsed-text reviewed because the targeted screenshot failed. The notice does not establish that strike-off occurred, nor current corporate status. (S82; C79.)
+
+## 1. The missing ICIJ rows were a selection gap
+
+The recovered raw ZIP matches the baseline SHA256 `9e5faeed998b5715157bbdb2a600681d1b5bda259ba004c998b3b2836d3c49aa`, contains `GENERATED_ON_20260909.txt`, and passes the legacy six-member verifier. The three additions below were already present in that identical snapshot:
+
+| Raw CSV record | Holder node | Literal start date | Difference from old selection |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| 336664 | Equalchance12099846 | 29-DEC-2011 | Previously unselected; distinct from12163340 |
+| 336663 | Equalchance12099846 | 12-DEC-2012 | Previously unselected parallel row |
+| 466882 | Freegain12146270 | 12-DEC-2012 | Previously unselected neighbor |
+
+Atinia's 29-DEC-2011 row640470 was already OE08. It is not a new discovery. The resulting eleven Gorlane shareholder rows are recorded separately in [the ownership timeline](gorlane_ownership_timeline.csv). Blank end dates do not establish ownership today; parallel entries do not prove replacement or percentage holdings. The export-generation date is not the historical coverage date of the Panama Papers. (S59/S78; C73.)
+
+### Correction: the page hash was not identical
+
+The earlier substantive checkpoint says the recovered Gorlane HTML matched the previously logged hash `2ffa2b64942e6e0b1e1ef3da20c1967bad1ffe3b7b923dba2fc5055052f96b95`. **That assertion was not reproduced in this continuation.** The recovered page has SHA256 `ba9b2708579f1165ae031782e9a89f8cae34b889e8cc75dc6d5e6900e2d659b4`, matching its own acquisition record but not that earlier page hash. The baseline page bytes are unavailable for a detailed HTML diff. The cause is therefore undetermined. All eleven shareholder rows in the recovered page agree with the unchanged export. Different HTML bytes do not, by themselves, establish a changed ownership record or snapshot. (S77/S78; C86.)
+
+### What “complete scoped review” means here
+
+Every relationship incident in either direction to the fifteen explicit handoff seeds was retained, including previously unknown opposite endpoints, then hydrated across all five node-type files. The scope contains40 distinct raw relationships and39 hydrated endpoints. All55 visible relationship-table projections across the15 recovered pages map to raw rows; the same relationship can appear on more than one page. These counts describe coverage, not forty financial transactions or fifty-five independent confirmations.
+
+The actual name/original-name/former-name query produced57 candidates, each retained with an acceptance, unresolved, custody-only or rejection reason. Unresolved aliases were not silently promoted to resolved seeds. The full result, preservation method, date discipline and qualified identity decisions are in [the neighborhood review](gorlane_neighborhood_review.md). ICIJ pages, raw CSV and API are one evidence family. Its public graph is not the underlying leaked-document cache or a bank ledger. Offshore incorporation or inclusion in the database is not evidence of wrongdoing.
+
+## 2. What the original documents corroborate—and challenge
+
+The UK return independently corroborates a named shareholder/subsidiary leg, but its accounts challenge any automatic assumption that this UK company was the visible conduit for the purchase price. The image-reviewed 2009 and2010 dormant accounts report GBP2 net assets; the2009 balance sheet explicitly shows zero cash at bank/in hand. These are filed company representations, not a bank audit. They do not settle whether another company, a nominee arrangement or a different accounting perimeter carried the investment. The UK charge page reports zero registered charges for this company, not zero security across the group or in Cyprus. The2011–2013 accounts were recovered but not freshly image-reviewed in this continuation; no new accounting conclusion here rests on them. (S97/S92/S99; C77.)
+
+The return dates must also stay separate from officer changes. The2010 return is made up to23July2010. The two TM01 forms report director terminations on28July2010, but the filings were received on different dates: Shnaider28July and Chifrin5August. Those ordinary corporate-administration dates do not establish a May19 cash movement or coordination. Corporate-secretary records supply possible custodians, not financial beneficiaries. (S94/S95/S96; S93, physical3.)
+
+## 3. Judicial findings: stronger custody and a specific form of leverage
+
+The official Ukrainian publication of ruling110589321 was recovered. It is the original publication of the already indexed21April2023 interim-restraint proceeding, not an independent second case. The prosecution's account names the five Cyprus vehicles in a2010–2012 credit/pledge case. Its aggregate figure exceedingUSD940million is neither an original facility nor proven advances, and is not additional purchase proceeds. The judgment distinguishes the restraint assessment from final determination of guilt. (S73/S80; C80.)
+
+The English judgment's important analytical distinction remains intact: Etmor's USD105.825million payment benefited Parborio's obligation; the court preferred discharge of an existing settlement obligation over the competing loan characterization. A more focused follow-through concerns paragraph927: VEB is described as negotiating the2012Quinira exit and requiring no-claim assurances from Etmor. **That is transaction-specific contractual leverage, not merely recurring institutional proximity.** Its scope must not expand into proof of the2010payment source or universal control. The later netting/novation drafts remain unsigned. The executed exit terms, release and VEB consent are still missing. (S71, paragraphs902–904,919–932; C82.)
+
+The full acquired *Dargamo v Avonwick*, [2021]EWCA Civ1149, concerns the limited unjust-enrichment appeal over the Castlerose transaction. It dismisses that appeal without adopting every aspect of the trial reasoning. It is not an appellate adjudication of every Gorlane finding; no separately sealed order or separate appeal specifically challenging paragraphs902–932 was obtained. (S79; C81.)
+
+The [purchaser/financing-role matrix](gorlane_purchaser_roles.csv) consequently leaves the three unnamed VEB-associated purchasers unmapped. Belego, Sileni and Equalchance are not certified as those three by subtracting Parborio and Atinia from a five-name graph list. The newly obtained identifiers make that proposition testable through particular registries; they do not prove it.
+
+## 4. Where the proceeds and Toronto obligations now stand
+
+No acquired record in this continuation closes a seller-proceeds-to-Toronto payment leg. That is a result of the particular documents reviewed, not a claim that no such record exists. The new corporate filings establish ownership observations; the official identifier annex establishes precise research targets; neither establishes a receiving account or application of sale proceeds.
+
+The Toronto record is better framed as a separate obligation reconciliation. Wolf's physical47–48 reports, through counsel, CAD105,651,246 principal plus accrued interest due as of30September2016, and a separate CAD9,315,000 guarantee fee. The2007charge's CAD200million face is not an advance. The reported on/about6June2011MRHL–MDI assignment remains a noncash legal event unless its deed, debt schedule and consideration records establish more. Unknown principal, consideration and repayments are not zero. (S18, paragraphs71–75; S37.)
+
+Physical55, paragraphs98–101, explains why receiver-only accounts cannot close the operational accounting scope: the contemplated arrangement leaves respondent cash/receivables and management operations outside the receiver's own operating account perimeter. Paragraph100 calls for respondent biweekly receipts/disbursements and bank reconciliations. The actual reports and agent application postings—not simply additional receiver account totals—are the next relevant documents. Their delivery or contents were not established here. (S18; C85.)
+
+A separate Ontario lead describes a2019assignment of judgments from Guernsey Midland to a legally separate BVI Midland and identifies a certificate/transfer-instrument trail. Its court-file and annex locators are now recorded, but it is not the2011Toronto assignment and does not certify that the BVI assignee is ICIJ10088926. The available review is of web-indexed judicial reproductions; the annexes and transfer instrument were not acquired. (S100–S102; C83.)
+
+The [cash/obligation table](gorlane_cash_obligation_reconciliation.csv) keeps purchase price, tranche amounts, Etmor payment, settlement obligation, rounded presentation figures, security face, principal-plus-interest, guarantee fee, assignment and2017reported benefit distinct. It also retains a separate economic-dependence inquiry: whether financing sustained project or contractual value requires timing, liquidity needs, alternatives, actual use and causation. A later disclosed benefit cannot substitute for those records; absence of a wire does not erase genuine contractual leverage.
+
+## 5. The tax locating candidate is now supported—but only at its actual reporting level
+
+Only the existing tax-C artifact was recovered; the53-PDF/6,340-page inventory was not reacquired as a new discovery. The selected106-page2017parent package matches TR31/S63's SHA256 `63c9519f0c9ada783dfecf8f9b4e3d29c99ce465252f5552adead9b8fee80d82`.
+
+**Physical38, TY2017 GenDep, expressly names the Toronto manager and amount2,219,431.** The paragraph says the named QSubs filed their own NYC-3L forms and reported taxable income being excluded from “this return.” It separately states Vancouver133,677 and total2,353,108. The internal arithmetic agrees; that is not a reconciliation to OGE. The attachment is headed as Section108(i) information and also describes a Fortress/401Mezz debt modification. Its form/line-reference field is blank. We do not assign the Toronto amount to a specific Form1120S line or infer that the debt description concerns the Toronto manager. Neighboring physical37 and39 contain different schedules and do not supply a manager-level allocation. (S63, physical37–39; C84; Q12.)
+
+This newly read source supplies the entity, tax year, physical-page locator and bounded meaning required before reviving the old locating candidate. **Q12 records a named taxable-income exclusion narrative, not gross receipts, cash, management fees, buyout consideration or a complete amendment explanation.** It does not replace Q01/R03's USD2,273,297 mixed reported economic benefit. No difference between the two measures is classified as expense, tax, distribution or termination consideration. The source wording is retained without relying on unacquired historical NYC instructions.
+
+The reported19January2017QSub election and possible1–18January interval remain distinct from an accepted election. The subsidiary trial balance, own NYC-3L, parent elimination schedules, original-versus-amended comparison, actual election/acceptance and executed buyout allocation remain unacquired. The27June2017announcement is a real commercial event but still does not disclose price, completion consideration or the exact remitter/recipient allocation. (S63/S69/S74; Q01/Q02/Q10–Q12.)
+
+## 6. Cumulative assessment and remaining boundary
+
+The cumulative evidence is more specific than an unexplained recurrence of VEB: a judicially described acquisition/payment mechanism; a later, transaction-specific conditional negotiating role; historical offshore shareholder entries; a corroborated UK subsidiary holding; and identifiable companies in official records. It justifies pursuing the named instruments and counterparties rather than restarting a general association search. The contrary evidence and limits remain consequential: inconsistent UK transfer dates, small dormant-company accounts, unsigned proposals, interim rather than final findings, and separate legal/reporting entities.
+
+Nothing here establishes Trump knowledge of a particular financing origin, political coordination, a continuous VEB–A7 payment channel, or revived RF-014. A7's later history, Exim/Aragon identity and custody questions, and diesel/ICC policy are unchanged and outside this transaction wave.
+
+This continuation delivers the scoped review, ledgers, chronology, reconciliation, precise unsent records requests and narrow tests. It does not claim acquisition of the executed May2010SPA/buyer schedule, Etmor instructions, VEB original credit/charge instruments, cash-flow exhibits,2012executed exit/release,2011MRHL–MDI deed or2017buyout allocation. Public access failures and reproduction limits are itemized in [the search log](gorlane_search_log.md). Publication and actual check results are recorded separately in the delivery/validation record; a green structural test is not historical authentication.
